onsequence updating, coherence accounting, collapse/recovery dynamics, closure governance, and a final ERC index to evaluate whether an autonomous system preserves continuity under disruption. Across simulated architecture classes, the integrated continuity engine produced the highest fracture tolerance, increasing the critical fracture threshold beyond self-model-only and recovery-augmented architectures. Ablation analysis identified recovery dynamics, recovery history, coherence signature, future trajectory, and consequence history as the dominant contributors to continuity preservation, while memory alone functioned as a supportive but non-primary factor. The work provides a bounded computational framework for studying autonomous identity persistence, AI stability, recovery under fracture, and continuity-sensitive system evaluation."}, {"id": "https:

